-- title:
--   Point heights of a base-point-free subfamily differ boundedly
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \geq 1$ and write $\bar F_N$ for [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111), the subfield of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the coefficientwise images of the field [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305) $= \mathbb{Q}(\text{divisorExpansions } N) \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$. Let $s : \mathrm{Fin}\,a \to \bar F_N$ and $u : \mathrm{Fin}\,b \to \bar F_N$ be finite families with $s_i \neq 0$ for all $i$ and $u_j \neq 0$ for all $j$, and let $D$ be a divisor, that is, a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on the places of $\bar F_N$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, whose degree satisfies $\deg D \geq 2g + 1$, where $g =$ `genusFF` is the $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-dimension of $H^1(0)$. Assume the $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-span of the range of $s$ is exactly the Riemann–Roch space $L(D) = \{f : v(f) \leq \exp(D\,v) \text{ for every place } v\}$, that each $u_j$ lies in $L(D)$, and that for every place $w$ there is an index $j$ with $\operatorname{ord}_w(u_j) + D\,w = 0$, where $\operatorname{ord}_w = -\log \circ w$ on the adic valuation. Then there is a real constant $C$ such that for every place $v$ of $\bar F_N$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ one has $|\,\mathrm{pointHt}\,s\,v - \mathrm{pointHt}\,u\,v\,| \leq C$, where $\mathrm{pointHt}$ is the absolute logarithmic height (normalised by the degree of the field generated by the coordinates) of the vector obtained by evaluating at $v$ the quotients of the family members by the pivot member at $v$.
--
--   This is the first comparison step of Weil's height machine in the height currency used throughout this development: the height attached to the complete linear system $L(D)$ and the height attached to a base-point-free subfamily of it agree up to $O(1)$, uniformly in the place. It is used in the subsequent comparisons of $\mathrm{pointHt}$ with the absolute height of $j$-coordinates and with the heights of regularised values on $J_0(N)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_JZero_exists_abs_pointHt_sub_pointHt_le_of_forall_exists_ord_add_eq_zero.lean

